try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in)) {
    System.out.print('Enter age: ');
    while (true) {
        String input = sc.nextLine().trim();
        try {
            int age = Integer.parseInt(input);
            if (age >= 1 && age <= 150) {
                System.out.println('Age: ' + age);
                break;
            }
            System.out.print('Age must be 1-150. Try again: ');
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.print('Invalid number. Try again: ');
        }
    }
}

Improvements: (1) try-with-resources auto-closes Scanner. (2) Read as nextLine() + parseInt() — avoids buffer bug. (3) Validation loop with range check. (4) Graceful error messages.

public static String readEmail(Scanner sc) {
    String emailRegex = '^[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$';
    while (true) {
        System.out.print('Enter email: ');
        String input = sc.nextLine().trim();
        if (input.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println('Email cannot be empty.');
        } else if (!input.matches(emailRegex)) {
            System.out.println('Invalid email format. Example: user@domain.com');
        } else {
            return input;
        }
    }
}

Key points: (1) Read as nextLine() — handles full email with dots/plus. (2) trim() removes leading/trailing spaces. (3) Empty check before regex. (4) Clear error with example. (5) Loop until valid — method only returns on success. This pattern is reusable for any validated string input.

