private static final Scanner SC = new Scanner(System.in);

public static int readInt(String prompt, int min, int max) {
    while (true) {
        System.out.print(prompt);
        String raw = SC.nextLine().trim();
        if (raw.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println('Input cannot be empty.');
            continue;
        }
        try {
            int val = Integer.parseInt(raw);
            if (val < min || val > max) {
                System.out.printf('Enter a value between %d and %d.%n', min, max);
            } else {
                return val;
            }
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println('\'' + raw + '\' is not a valid integer.');
        }
    }
}

Design decisions: (1) Static Scanner — one instance, no conflicts. (2) nextLine() + parseInt() — no buffer bug. (3) trim() + empty check. (4) Range validation after type validation. (5) Clear, specific error messages. (6) Only returns on valid input — caller always gets a valid int.
